(a) An ordinance, resolution, or contract may be enacted either at a regular or a special meeting.
(b) The mayor shall sign, as a ministerial and administrative act, and with no discretion, all ordinances, resolutions, contracts, and other official documents approved by the city council within three (3) business days of their approval by the city council and shall deliver each executed document to the city clerk. The mayor's execution of such documents shall not constitute approval, veto authority, discretionary review, or authority to delay implementation of any action lawfully approved by the city council. Nothing herein shall be construed to alter or limit the authority of the city manager or any other authorized official to execute contracts or documents as otherwise permitted by the Charter, this Code, the city's purchasing policy, or applicable law.
(c) For purposes of this section, the mayor shall be deemed "unable to sign" or "unable to act" only in the event of:
(1) Physical or mental incapacity;
(2) Illness or medical emergency;
(3) Death;
(4) Absence from the state preventing timely execution;
(5) Military service or emergency governmental duty;
(6) Suspension, disqualification, or legal prohibition from acting in the capacity of mayor; or
(7) Vacancy in the office of mayor.
(d) No later than twenty-four (24) hours following each regular or special meeting of the city council, the city clerk shall publish on the city's website a list of all ordinances, resolutions, and contracts approved by the city council. The city clerk shall promptly notify the mayor pro tem and each member of city council whether the mayor has signed such ordinances, resolutions, and contracts within three (3) business days of their approval, as required herein.
(Code 1963, § 2-2; Ord. No. 2024-10, § 1, 5-22-24; Ord. No. 2026-12, § 1(Exh. A), 5-18-26)

